From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] phy: allwinner: A733 combo-PHY -- latch the Type-C plug direction in reset

COMB0's plug direction is LINK_CTRL0 bit12 TYPEC_CONN_DIR, and the UM
lets it change only with the PHY in reset: the field has to stay static
while phy0_reset_n is de-asserted (UM V1.00 18.2.6.6, PDF p. 1175;
phy0_reset_n is bit0 of the same register, PDF p. 1176). The Type-C
switch callback changed it on a running PHY on every orientation change
after phy_init().

WHERE IT CHANGED LIVE, READ IN THE CODE
---------------------------------------

  - a733_combophy_typec_set() re-ran a733_combophy_param_config()
    whenever dwc3 held the PHY, and param_config() writes bit12 by
    read-modify-write in the middle of the PMA table. On the USB side no
    reset followed. dwc3 calls phy_init() at probe, before TCPM attaches,
    and the probe default is NORMAL, so every reversed plug after boot
    took this path, and every change of direction after that.
  - With DP active the callback re-latched the direction through the DP
    staircase, but only after the same live table write.
  - A re-init after phy_exit() finds phy0_reset_n still released,
    because phy_exit() only powers the PHY down, and param_config()
    rewrites bit12 before reset_seq() pulses the reset. That is a change
    only if the plug was turned while nobody held the PHY; in the same
    orientation the write repeats the value.

WHAT SILICON SAYS SO FAR, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
----------------------------------------------

The live flip works. SuperSpeed came up at 10000 after it on the 8 GiB
board (2026-08-19, normal -> reverse, LINK_CTRL0 0x00001111) and
on the 6 GiB board (2026-09-24, reverse -> normal).

It does not say which write carried the flip. The same param_config()
call also rewrites the orientation forks of the PMA table, the lane
blocks mirrored n -> 3-n (^0x600): 82 PMA words differ between the two
directions, and they move the transmit and receive setup from lanes 0/1
to 3/2. The UM names the two USB lane candidates -- "COMB0 PHY Lane0 or
Lane3 PIPE CLK" (0x6100) and "Lane1 or Lane2 PIPE RX_DATA" (0x6104), PDF
p. 1197 -- but not what picks between them; bit12 is the only
orientation field in the wrapper. Three readings fit the two live flips:

  - bit12 acts on a running PHY;
  - bit12 does not pick the USB lanes, and the forks carry the flip;
  - bit12 is taken at a power-state change -- the P3 -> P0 of a connect
    -- rather than only at reset release.

Nothing measured separates them. A script that toggles the direction
tests the first against a standing U0 link.

WHAT THIS DOES
--------------

  - a733_combophy_hold_reset(): LINK_CTRL0, DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL and
    LINK_CTRL3 back to their reset value 0 (UM V1.00 18.2.6.6, 18.2.6.9,
    18.2.6.25), with power and clocks left on. LINK_CTRL0 goes down in
    two writes, every reset first with the direction kept, then the
    direction: one write of 0 from a reversed plug would drop bit0 and
    bit12 on the same edge. LINK_CTRL3 goes too because a new
    power-state request needs the old one returned to 0, and the first
    request after reset release has to be A0 or A2 (18.2.6.9, PDF p. 1180).
    a733_combophy_dp_teardown() now calls it.
  - Switch callback, USB holds the PHY: a733_combophy_usb_relatch() --
    the hold, param_config() under it, reset_seq(). That is phy_init()'s
    register sequence without its IDDQ and clock steps. It prints
    LINK_CTRL0 before and after, LINK_STUS0 and PMA_CMN_READY.
  - Switch callback, DP holds the PHY: the hold moves in front of the
    table write. The DP staircase after it is unchanged; it began with
    every reset low anyway.
  - Both branches of the callback now need cp->inited. phy_exit() clears
    it and leaves dp_active standing, and the DP branch wrote the PMA and
    ran the staircase with or without the clocks.
  - bringup(): the hold before IDDQ is cleared, so a re-init starts where
    the first init starts. On the first init the three registers already
    read 0.
  - param_config() warns if it is ever asked to change the direction
    with phy0_reset_n released.

One more UM rule is met on the way. param_config() writes LINK_CTRL1
absolutely, and REFCLK_DIG_SEL and the other reference-clock fields
have to be set before apb_preset_n/phy_reset_n are de-asserted (UM
V1.00 18.2.6.7, PDF pp. 1177-1178). The switch callback used to write it
into a running PHY, with the same value; it now lands under the hold.

WHY A RESET AND NOT THE EXIT/INIT CYCLE OF THE DP RETURN
--------------------------------------------------------

The UM asks for phy0_reset_n low while the bit changes, and for nothing
else. The exit/init cycle that
phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-hand-comb0-back-to-usb3-after-dp runs when
DP leaves adds IDDQ and a clock cycle, and does not assert the reset by
itself: phy_exit()'s body leaves LINK_CTRL0 alone, and on that path the
DP teardown is what holds it. The reset is the part of that cycle the UM
requires. The DP return is left as it is.

UPSTREAM
--------

The three mainline drivers read for this write their Type-C lane select
only under reset. qmp_combo_com_init() writes DP_COM_TYPEC_CTRL with
SW_USB3PHY_RESET held, and qmp_combo_typec_switch_set() reaches it
through com_exit()/com_init() with dwc3 left running
(drivers/phy/qualcomm/phy-qcom-qmp-combo.c). rk_udphy_init() writes the
lane mux after rk_udphy_reset_assert_all(), and a live orientation
change only records the mapping for the next init
(drivers/phy/rockchip/phy-rockchip-usbdp.c). wiz_phy_reset_deassert()
sets the lane swap of TI's wrapper around a Cadence PHY right before
phy_reset_n (drivers/phy/ti/phy-j721e-wiz.c). Code pattern only; no
value comes from them.

WHAT DWC3 SEES
--------------

tcpm_src_attach() switches VBUS on and only then sets the orientation
(tcpm_set_vbus(), then tcpm_set_roles() -> tcpm_mux_set() ->
typec_set_orientation()). A bus-powered device is still starting, and
the SuperSpeed port has no link to lose. A self-powered device can
present its SuperSpeed terminations before VBUS and before TCPM's
debounce, so its link may start training in the old direction; the
relatch takes it down and it trains again. The live write had the same
race, so this is not a regression, but it is a case the bus-powered
enclosure does not exercise.

The PHY, its PIPE side included, is in reset for the few milliseconds of
the handshake, and dwc3 keeps running. That is where this can fall
short, and the UM says why. For bit 0 of the same register the UM
requires all PHY inputs to follow the PIPE specification's PIPE-reset
levels when reset is released (UM V1.00 18.2.6.6, PDF p. 1176). A
dwc3 rebind meets that, because the controller is reset before the PHY
leaves reset; this release does not, dwc3 drives the PIPE inputs from
whatever state its port is in. And with PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_USB3P1_MASK =
0 and PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT = 1, as reset_seq() leaves them, the PIPE
link reset is the one USB3P1 outputs to PHY0 (bit 5, same
page), a line the controller drives and this driver cannot pulse. The DP
return of phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-hand-comb0-back-to-usb3-after-dp
carries the same limit. Hence the first falsifier below.

THE VENDOR
----------

Read as a hint (bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c,
the radxa/allwinner-bsp tree): sunxi_cadence_combo0_usb_phy_set_mode() re-runs
combo0_usb_param_config() on an orientation change with no reset
handshake, and combo0_configure_usb_dp() writes TYPEC_CONN_DIR by
read-modify-write inside it. This callback had ported that; the UM rule
is what the vendor leaves out. Nothing here is taken from it.

THE PROOF THIS OWES
-------------------

Test kernel with this patch, localversion -861conndir, an RTL9210 Gen 2
enclosure on the Type-C port, instrumented by a script:

  1. Plug turned over after boot: the new line with PMA_CMN_READY set
     and LINK_CTRL0 at its want value, the device at 10000 on the
     SuperSpeed bus, PORTSC2 in U0, forks and bit12 agree.
  2. dwc3 rebind in the same orientation: the same, no param_config()
     warning.
  3. Transaction errors of the enclosure in the states of 1 and 2 no
     worse than on the default kernel.
  4. With the DP return patch in the same kernel: DP sink in orientation
     X, unplug, the enclosure plugged in X, then in the other
     orientation. 10000 both times. X gives the DP return's line alone;
     the other orientation gives the DP return's line and then this
     patch's relatch line. That second case no longer proves the DP
     return on its own, because the relatch releases PHY_RESET_N on the
     same path; the DP return is proven on a kernel without this patch.
  5. If a self-powered SuperSpeed device is at hand: 1 with it.

Falsifiers: 480M with PMA_CMN_READY set (the controller does not follow
a PHY reset under it and needs its own), PMA_CMN_READY NOT set (the hold
lacks something the first init has), or a param_config() warning (a
caller writes the direction outside the hold). On the first, unbind and
bind xhci-hcd.1.auto alone before anything else -- an HCRST with no
CSFTRST and no PHY cycle -- and only then dwc3, the same separation the
DP return names.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
diff -u a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
--- a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
@@ -607,16 +607,51 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * Hold COMB0 in reset with its power and clocks left on: the three link
+ * control registers the release handshakes drive, back at their reset value
+ * 0x00000000 (UM V1.00 18.2.6.6, 18.2.6.9 and 18.2.6.25, PDF pp. 1175, 1179 and
+ * 1196).
+ *
+ * This is the only state in which TYPEC_CONN_DIR may change. LINK_CTRL0 bit12
+ * has to stay static while phy0_reset_n is de-asserted (18.2.6.6, PDF
+ * p. 1175), and phy0_reset_n is bit0 of the same register (PDF p. 1176).
+ * LINK_CTRL0 is lowered in two writes: first every reset with the direction
+ * kept, then the direction. A single write of 0 from a reversed plug
+ * (0x00001111) would drop bit0 and bit12 on the same edge, and the UM does
+ * not say which side of that edge the direction is sampled on.
+ * LINK_CTRL3 goes back to 0 with it: pma_xcvr_power_state_req has to be
+ * acknowledged and set back to 0 before the next state is requested, and
+ * the first request after reset
+ * release has to be A0 or A2 (18.2.6.9, PDF p. 1180). Both release
+ * paths request A0 again: a733_combophy_reset_seq() for USB,
+ * a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq() for DP. Caller holds cp->lock.
+ */
+static void a733_combophy_hold_reset(struct a733_combophy *cp)
+{
+	u32 dir = readl(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0) & PHY0_TYPEC_CONN_DIR;
+
+	writel(dir, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
+	writel(0, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
+	writel(0, cp->top2 + DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL);
+	writel(0, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL3);
+	usleep_range(DP_RESET_STEP_MIN_US, DP_RESET_STEP_MAX_US);
+}
+
+/*
  * combo0_usb_param_config() -> combo0_configure_usb_dp(), orientation NORMAL,
  * ssc_en = 0. The three PMA blocks are split by the two non-PMA writes the
  * vendor performs in the middle of the sequence, and the order is load-bearing:
  * LINK_CTRL1 is written as an absolute value after the CMN/lane-0-1 block, and
  * TYPEC_CONN_DIR is cleared before the lane-2/3 block, not at the end.
+ *
+ * Every caller holds the PHY in reset across this -- a733_combophy_hold_reset()
+ * -- and releases it afterwards; see the direction write below.
  */
 static void a733_combophy_param_config(struct a733_combophy *cp)
 {
 	bool flip = cp->orientation == TYPEC_ORIENTATION_REVERSE;
 	u16 m = flip ? PMA_LANE_MIRROR : 0;
+	u32 ctrl0;
 
 	a733_pma_write_all(cp, a733_pma_a_common, ARRAY_SIZE(a733_pma_a_common), 0);
 	a733_pma_write_all(cp, a733_pma_fork1, ARRAY_SIZE(a733_pma_fork1), m);
@@ -629,8 +664,17 @@
 	a733_pma_write_all(cp, a733_pma_b_post, ARRAY_SIZE(a733_pma_b_post), 0);
 
 	/* LINK_CTRL0 bit12 TYPEC_CONN_DIR: 1 = flipped plug. UM-derived field,
-	 * (A733 user manual).
+	 * (A733 user manual). It may change only while
+	 * phy0_reset_n is low (UM V1.00 18.2.6.6, PDF p. 1175); the callers see
+	 * to that. A caller that ever reaches this with the PHY out of reset
+	 * and the direction about to change is named here, not left silent.
 	 */
+	ctrl0 = readl(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
+	if ((ctrl0 & PHY0_RESET_N) &&
+	    !!(ctrl0 & PHY0_TYPEC_CONN_DIR) != flip)
+		dev_warn(cp->dev,
+			 "TYPEC_CONN_DIR changes while PHY_RESET_N is released (LINK_CTRL0 0x%08x); UM 18.2.6.6 wants it static\n",
+			 ctrl0);
 	a733_rmw(cp->link, COMB0_LINK_CTRL0, PHY0_TYPEC_CONN_DIR,
 		 flip ? PHY0_TYPEC_CONN_DIR : 0);
 
@@ -1542,17 +1586,15 @@
 
 /*
  * combo0_dp_phy_reset(deassert = false). Three absolute zeroes, in the vendor's
- * order. Returning POWER_STATE_REQ to 0 is not housekeeping: UM 18.2.6.8 says
- * pma_xcvr_power_state_req has to be acknowledged and set back to 0 before the
- * next state is requested, so leaving
- * A0 standing would block the next entry.
+ * order. Returning POWER_STATE_REQ to 0 is not housekeeping: UM V1.00 18.2.6.9
+ * (PDF p. 1180) wants pma_xcvr_power_state_req acknowledged and set back
+ * to 0 before the next state is requested, so leaving A0 standing would
+ * block the next entry. The
+ * writes are a733_combophy_hold_reset(), the hold the USB side uses as well.
  */
 static void a733_combophy_dp_teardown(struct a733_combophy *cp)
 {
-	writel(0, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
-	writel(0, cp->top2 + DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL);
-	writel(0, cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL3);
-	usleep_range(DP_RESET_STEP_MIN_US, DP_RESET_STEP_MAX_US);
+	a733_combophy_hold_reset(cp);
 }
 
 static void a733_combophy_assert_reset(void *data)
@@ -1655,6 +1697,13 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto err_dcxo;
 
+	/* In reset before the PHY leaves IDDQ, as on the first init, where the
+	 * three registers still hold their reset values and this rewrites them.
+	 * A re-init after phy_exit() finds phy0_reset_n released -- exit only
+	 * powers down -- and param_config() below writes the direction, a
+	 * change if the plug was turned while nobody held the PHY.
+	 */
+	a733_combophy_hold_reset(cp);
 	a733_combophy_power_up(cp);
 	a733_combophy_clk_route(cp);
 	a733_combophy_param_config(cp);
@@ -1715,15 +1764,77 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * A new plug direction on a PHY that USB3 holds: hold it in reset, write the
+ * direction and the orientation forks of the PMA table under the reset, and
+ * release it through the handshake phy_init() ends with. That is phy_init()'s
+ * register sequence without its power and clock steps; the UM asks for the
+ * reset, not for those, and they stay as they are. dwc3 is not reset.
+ *
+ * The line it prints carries LINK_CTRL0 before and after and PMA_CMN_READY, so
+ * a session can tell a PHY that did not come back from a controller that did
+ * not follow it. Caller holds cp->lock and has checked cp->inited.
+ */
+static void a733_combophy_usb_relatch(struct a733_combophy *cp)
+{
+	bool flip = cp->orientation == TYPEC_ORIENTATION_REVERSE;
+	u32 before, want, stus;
+
+	before = readl(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0);
+	a733_combophy_hold_reset(cp);
+	a733_combophy_param_config(cp);
+	/* reports its own stalls; it has no failure to hand back */
+	a733_combophy_reset_seq(cp);
+
+	want = PHY0_RESET_N | PHY0_PIPE_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT |
+	       PHY0_DP_LINK_RESET_N_SOFT | (flip ? PHY0_TYPEC_CONN_DIR : 0);
+	stus = readl(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_STUS0);
+	dev_info(cp->dev,
+		 "orientation %s: TYPEC_CONN_DIR re-latched through PHY_RESET_N: LINK_CTRL0 0x%08x -> 0x%08x LINK_STUS0 0x%08x (want 0x%08x, PMA_CMN_READY %s)\n",
+		 flip ? "reverse" : "normal", before,
+		 readl(cp->link + COMB0_LINK_CTRL0), stus, want,
+		 stus & PHY0_PMA_CMN_READY ? "set" : "NOT set");
+}
+
+/*
  * TCPM hands us the plug orientation through the Type-C switch framework. The
  * ordering is fixed and awkward: combophy0 probes, dwc3 probes and calls
  * phy_init(), and only then does the TCPC attach and report which way round the
  * cable is. dwc3 never re-inits the PHY, so caching the value is not enough --
  * a port that is already up has to be reprogrammed in place.
  *
- * That is safe here: the vendor does exactly this on an orientation change and
- * nothing more -- combo0_usb_param_config() alone, no reset pulse, no clock
- * cycle (sunxi-cadence-combophy.c:3197).
+ * In place means through reset. The direction is LINK_CTRL0 bit12, which has to
+ * stay static while phy0_reset_n is de-asserted (UM V1.00 18.2.6.6, PDF
+ * p. 1175). The vendor ignores that: on an orientation change it re-runs
+ * combo0_usb_param_config() on the running PHY, and the direction is a plain
+ * read-modify-write inside it, with no reset around it
+ * (sunxi_cadence_combo0_usb_phy_set_mode(), combo0_configure_usb_dp()). This
+ * callback used to port exactly that. Now:
+ *
+ *  - USB owns the lanes: a733_combophy_usb_relatch() -- hold, direction and
+ *    PMA forks, release.
+ *  - DP owns them: the same hold, the same table, then the DP staircase as
+ *    before. The staircase begins with every reset low anyway; the hold moves
+ *    that point in front of the direction write instead of behind it.
+ *  - Nobody holds the PHY: only the cache moves, and the next phy_init()
+ *    writes the direction inside its own hold.
+ *
+ * The three mainline drivers read for this write their lane select only under
+ * reset, never into a running PHY: qmp_combo_com_init() writes
+ * DP_COM_TYPEC_CTRL with SW_USB3PHY_RESET held, and qmp_combo_typec_switch_set()
+ * gets there through a com_exit()/com_init() cycle with dwc3 left running;
+ * rk_udphy_init() writes the lane mux after rk_udphy_reset_assert_all();
+ * wiz_phy_reset_deassert() sets the lane swap of TI's wrapper around a Cadence
+ * PHY right before it releases phy_reset_n.
+ *
+ * What dwc3 sees: TCPM switches VBUS on and then sets the orientation
+ * (tcpm_src_attach(): tcpm_set_vbus(), then tcpm_set_roles() -> tcpm_mux_set()
+ * -> typec_set_orientation()), so a bus-powered device is still starting and
+ * the SuperSpeed port has no link to lose. A self-powered device can present
+ * its SuperSpeed terminations before VBUS and before TCPM's debounce, so the
+ * link may start training in the old direction; the relatch then takes it
+ * down and it trains again. The live write had the same race. The PHY, its
+ * PIPE side included, is in reset for the few milliseconds of the handshake;
+ * the controller is not.
  */
 static int a733_combophy_typec_set(struct typec_switch_dev *sw,
 				   enum typec_orientation orientation)
@@ -1741,15 +1852,18 @@
 
 	mutex_lock(&cp->lock);
 	cp->orientation = orientation;
-	if (cp->inited)
-		a733_combophy_param_config(cp);
-	/* param_config() updates TYPEC_CONN_DIR with a plain read-modify-write,
-	 * which UM 18.2.6.5 forbids while phy0_reset_n is de-asserted. With DP
-	 * running the direction therefore has to be re-latched through the reset
-	 * staircase. Guarded by dp_active, so a USB-only port never gets here.
+	/* Both branches need the clocks phy_init() took. dp_active alone does
+	 * not say so: phy_exit() clears inited and leaves dp_active standing.
 	 */
-	if (cp->dp_active)
-		cp->dp_handshake_ok = !a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(cp);
+	if (cp->inited) {
+		if (cp->dp_active) {
+			a733_combophy_hold_reset(cp);
+			a733_combophy_param_config(cp);
+			cp->dp_handshake_ok = !a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(cp);
+		} else {
+			a733_combophy_usb_relatch(cp);
+		}
+	}
 	mutex_unlock(&cp->lock);
 
 	/* A non-zero return here aborts TCPM's attach, so failures are logged,
